    "body": "The CSS Working Group just discussed `[Draft] Introduce the new menu elements`.\n\n<details><summary>The full IRC log of that discussion</summary>\n&lt;jarhar> domfarolino: just want to re-raise this, i requested whatwg stage 2 for this<br>\n&lt;jarhar> domfarolino: we had some discussion with aria folks and tpac and the whatwg discussion<br>\n&lt;jarhar> domfarolino: i think that its ready for stage 2<br>\n&lt;jarhar> domfarolino: wanted to raise this here as well since theres some non-trivial ua styles<br>\n&lt;jarhar> domfarolino: most of these are based on the customizable select precedent<br>\n&lt;jarhar> domfarolino: hoping to get more feedback on the proposal so far<br>\n&lt;jarhar> astearns: does anyhone have questions before they read the pr?<br>\n&lt;jarhar> domfarolino: the explainer and issue are linked to from the PR<br>\n&lt;jarhar> astearns: there are a number of issues linked, is there a particular issue that is the main one?<br>\n&lt;smaug> Using fieldset the way the proposal does felt initially rather surprising, but not sure I like or dislike that :) It was just surprising.<br>\n&lt;jarhar> domfarolino: the first link in the PR description is the openui explainer, id take a look at that<br>\n&lt;jarhar> domfarolino: using fieldset seems reasonable to change<br>\n&lt;jarhar> domfarolino: this is one area i expect feedback on<br>\n&lt;jarhar> domfarolino: i think simon said something about it<br>\n&lt;jarhar> masonf: the alternative would be a new element like menuitemgroup<br>\n&lt;jarhar> domfarolino: yes<br>\n&lt;lwarlow> q+<br>\n&lt;jarhar> astearns: its probably more likely to get feedback on ua stylesheet if you raise a css issue<br>\n&lt;astearns> ack lwarlow<br>\n&lt;jarhar> lwarlow: it probably is answered if i read it, but theres mention of the orientation attribute. is that included or not? i think that bit probably needs quite a bit more discussion<br>\n&lt;jarhar> domfarolino: surface level in the spec and ua styles, but full implications on the element are not nailed down in the pr yet<br>\n&lt;jarhar> lwarlow: i think the existence of that attribute isnt necessarily the correct approach to go with<br>\n&lt;jarhar> lwarlow: theres the aria orientation, keyboard behavior, and ua styles that could match up in a way that is nice<br>\n&lt;jarhar> domfarolino: without being controlled by some other mechanism?<br>\n&lt;jarhar> lwarlow: if you have the ua stylesheet and then css changes layout for mobile, and then suddenly aria orientation might not match visual styles is a potential problem<br>\n&lt;jarhar> lwarlow: if we could control the orientation attr via css that would be great<br>\n&lt;jarhar> lwarlow: similar thing in range input discussion<br>\n&lt;jarhar> lwarlow: would be good to not come up with different decisions<br>\n&lt;jarhar> lwarlow: orentation as an attr should work on other controls too if we add it<br>\n&lt;jarhar> domfarolino: i wasnt aware of the other discussion, if you could point me to it or drop a comment that would be great<br>\n&lt;masonf> discussed for &lt;input type=range> also<br>\n&lt;RRSAgent> I have made the request to generate https://www.w3.org/2026/01/08-css-minutes.html fantasai<br>\n&lt;astearns> zakim, end meeting<br>\n</details>\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
